/T (Pearson's correlation between alcohol and quality is r = +0.476 \(p < 0.001\). The direction is positive: wines with more alcohol tend to receive higher scores. The strength is moderate, and r2 = 0.23 means alcohol alone explains about 23% of the variation in quality. With 1599 samples even small correlations are significant, so the size of r matters more than the p-value. This is an association, not proof that raising alcohol improves a wine.)
