/T (Interpretation of Figure 2. From quality 5 upwards the median alcohol rises steadily: about 9.7 \(quality 5\), 10.5 \(6\), 11.5 \(7\) and 12.2 \(8\), which agrees with the positive correlation. At the low end the pattern is not steady \(qualities 3 and 4 have medians of about 9.9 and 10.0, above quality 5\), but those groups have only 10 and 53 wines. Using the 1.5 x IQR rule, quality 5 has the most outliers \(30, all high alcohol values between 11.4 and 14.9\), quality 6 has 5 high outliers \(13.6 to 14.0\) and quality 3 has one low value \(8.4\). So many high-alcohol wines still received only an average score, and the boxes of neighbouring scores overlap a lot, so alcohol alone cannot separate them cleanly.)
