150 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 55 0 R
/T (5.1 Measurements to focus on)
/E (5.1 Measurements to focus on)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
151 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (Alcohol is the strongest correlate of quality \(r = +0.48\), has the largest standardized coefficient \(+0.30\), a low VIF, and is cheap and accurate to measure. In this data about two thirds of wines below 10% alcohol are rated 5, while among wines at 11.5% or more only about 10% are rated 5 or lower, so a low reading is a useful flag. Volatile acidity is second \(r = -0.39, standardized coefficient -0.18\), has a low VIF and a clear meaning \(acetic acid, vinegar taint\), so a high reading is a sensible reason to hold a batch for tasting. Sulphates would be the next measurement to add.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
152 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 55 0 R
/T (5.2 Can the model replace sensory evaluation?)
/E (5.2 Can the model replace sensory evaluation?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
153 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 55 0 R
/T (5.3 Limitations)
/E (5.3 Limitations)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (1. Subjective, imbalanced and duplicated data. Each score is the median of at least three experts, so it contains human noise and taste that no chemical measurement can predict. About 83% of wines are rated 5 or 6 with very few 3s and 8s. The 240 duplicate rows may also make the test score slightly optimistic because the same row can appear in both train and test sets.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (2. Limited scope and a simple model. The data covers only red Vinho Verde from one region, with no information on grape, vineyard, vintage, brand or price, so results should not be generalised to other wines. Only linear regression and a single train-test split were used, so the numbers could change somewhat with another split or with non-linear models.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 55 0 R
/T (5.4 Conclusion)
/E (5.4 Conclusion)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (Alcohol \(positive\) and volatile acidity \(negative\) are the two attributes most associated with red wine quality. A linear regression on all 11 measurements reaches R2 of about 0.40 and RMSE of about 0.62 on unseen wines. That is useful for screening but not accurate enough to replace the sensory panel. Future work could try random forest or gradient boosting, treat the task as classification \(good vs not good\), use cross-validation, and compare with the white wine data.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8 10 9]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 55 0 R
/T (References and acknowledgement)
/E (References and acknowledgement)
