>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (P. Cortez, A. Cerdeira, F. Almeida, T. Matos and J. Reis. Modeling wine preferences by data mining from physicochemical properties. Decision Support Systems, Elsevier, 47\(4\):547-553, 2009.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (2)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (No. R = 0.40 leaves 60% of the variation unexplained. RMSE = 0.62 and MAE = 0.50 are large when commercial grades differ by one or two points. Only 57% of predictions are within 0.5 of the true score and about 11% are off by more than a full point. The model also cannot identify the best and worst wines, which are the ones the company cares about most. It is better used as a cheap first screening step to flag unusual batches, while the sensory panel makes the final decision.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
