/Title (Multicollinearity. A VIF above 5 deserves attention and above 10 is serious. Fixed acidity \(7.37\) and density \(6.02\) exceed 5, which fits the heatmap, but no VIF is above 10, so the problem is moderate. Alcohol \(2.93\) and volatile acidity \(1.81\) have low VIFs, so their coefficients can be interpreted with reasonable confidence: higher alcohol is associated with higher quality, and higher volatile acidity with lower quality, holding the other attributes fixed. The coefficients of fixed acidity and density should not be read literally because they are entangled with each other and with alcohol.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 165 0 R
/Next 233 0 R
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Title (Interpretation of Figure 5 and limitations. If the model were perfect all points would lie on the dashed line. Instead the cloud is flatter: predictions only range from 4.7 to 6.8, so the model never predicts a 3 or an 8. Poor wines are over-predicted \(actual 3s and 4s average about 5.1 to 5.2\) and the best wines are under-predicted \(actual 7s average about 6.2 and actual 8s about 6.6\). The scatter is wide at every level; only 57% of test wines are predicted within 0.5 of the true score and 89% within 1 point. Linear regression also assumes constant straight-line effects, cannot capture interactions or thresholds, and treats an ordered whole-number score as a continuous value.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 165 0 R
/Prev 232 0 R
>>
endobj
