/Title (Pearson's correlation between alcohol and quality is r = +0.476 \(p < 0.001\). The direction is positive: wines with more alcohol tend to receive higher scores. The strength is moderate, and r2 = 0.23 means alcohol alone explains about 23% of the variation in quality. With 1599 samples even small correlations are significant, so the size of r matters more than the p-value. This is an association, not proof that raising alcohol improves a wine.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 334 0 R
/Next 376 0 R
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Title (Interpretation of Figure 1. Scores only range from 3 to 8. Scores 5 \(681 wines\) and 6 \(638 wines\) make up about 83% of the data, while 3 and 8 are very rare \(10 and 18 wines\). The shape is roughly bell shaped and almost symmetric \(skewness 0.22\) but strongly peaked. The classes are imbalanced, so the model has very few examples of the best and worst wines to learn from.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 334 0 R
/Prev 375 0 R
>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<<
/Title (Metric MAE)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 231 0 R
/Prev 337 0 R
/Next 402 0 R
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Title (Explanation. The MAE of 0.50 means predictions are off by about half a quality point on average. The RMSE of 0.62 is a little larger because it penalises big errors more. R2 = 0.40 means the model explains only about 40% of the variation in quality of unseen wines; 60% is unexplained. Always predicting the training mean would give an RMSE of 0.81, so the model is better than guessing but not by a large margin. Train R2 \(0.35\) is slightly below test R2, so there is no sign of overfitting; the test split was probably a little easier, so a single split should not be over-trusted. On a scale where scores are mostly 5 or 6, an error of half a point is substantial.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 231 0 R
/Prev 403 0 R
/Next 338 0 R
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Title (Interpretation of Figure 4. Raw coefficients cannot be compared because the attributes have different units, so the features were standardized. The two most influential predictors are alcohol \(+0.297\) and volatile acidity \(-0.179\); sulphates \(+0.147\) is third. This matches the simple correlations in Section 3.3.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 338 0 R
>>
endobj
