400 0 obj
<<
/Title (Alcohol has a mean \(10.42\) slightly above its median \(10.2\) and a skewness of 0.86, so it is moderately right skewed. Volatile acidity has almost equal mean and median \(0.528 vs 0.52\) and skewness 0.67, so it is fairly symmetric with a small right tail. Residual sugar has a skewness of 4.54: a few wines with a lot of sugar \(maximum 15.5\) pull the mean \(2.54\) well above the median \(2.2\). For all three variables mean > median, which is the typical sign of positive skew.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 374 0 R
/Next 401 0 R
>>
endobj
401 0 obj
<<
/Title (Standard deviations cannot be compared directly because the variables are on different scales, so the coefficient of variation is used. Residual sugar has the greatest relative variability \(CV about 55.5%\), followed by volatile acidity \(33.9%\) and alcohol \(10.2%\). Alcohol has the largest standard deviation \(1.07\) only because it is measured on a bigger scale; relative to its mean it is the most stable.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 374 0 R
/Prev 400 0 R
>>
endobj
